        "+        $|f(x)-f(x')|\\le L\\,d(x,x')$ gives $f(x')\\ge f(x)-L\\,d(x,x')$. If $f(x)\\ge\\ell$ is known, safety means $f\\ge h$, and $L\\gt0$, every $x'$ with $d(x,x')\\le(\\ell-h)/L$ is certified without being evaluated. If $L=0$ and $\\ell\\ge h$, the function is constant on its domain and the whole domain is certified; use this case without dividing by zero. Example: $f(x_0)\\ge0.5$, threshold $0$, $L=2$ certify $|x'-x_0|\\le0.25$, and no more: $0.5-2|x'-x_0|$ is $2$-Lipschitz and hits $0$ there. If the true constant exceeds the assumed $L$, the certificate is void, not merely loose.",
