        "-        <em>Statement.</em> The state-action sequence satisfies $\\liminf_{T}\\frac1T\\sum_{t\\lt T}r_i(s_t,a_t)\\ge c_i$ almost surely for every constraint, and $\\lim_T\\mathbb E\\big[\\frac1T\\sum_{t\\lt T}r_0(s_t,a_t)\\big]\\ge P^\\star-\\eta_\\lambda B^2/2$ (the proof bounds the $\\liminf$, which is the safe reading if the limit does not exist).<br>",
        "+        <em>Setting.</em> During epoch $k$ the agent acts with $a_t\\sim\\pi(\\lambda_k)$, an <em>exact</em> maximiser of the average-reward Lagrangian for the current multipliers, and the multipliers follow the projected update above with fixed epoch length $T_0\\ge1$, step size $\\eta_\\lambda\\gt0$ and fixed nonnegative initial multipliers. (A learned $\\pi_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid s,\\lambda)$ only approximates $\\pi(\\lambda)$ and adds error terms not covered here.)<br>",
        "+        <em>Assumptions.</em> (A1) a strictly feasible policy with margin $C\\gt0$; (A2) $|r_i(s,a)-c_i|\\le B$ for every state, action and constraint $i$, as printed; with $m$ constraints this bounds the squared Euclidean norm of the epoch-averaged violation vector by $mB^2$; (A3) the epoch averages are unbiased given the current multipliers, $\\mathbb E\\big[\\frac1{T_0}\\sum_{t=kT_0}^{(k+1)T_0-1}r_i(s_t,a_t)\\,\\big|\\,\\lambda_k\\big]=V_i(\\pi(\\lambda_k))$ (the same is assumed for the integrable objective epoch averages of $r_0$; all rewards and the multiplier process are measurable). A rollout that starts from an arbitrary state is in general biased for a long-run average; the paper's Appendix II treats that case.<br>",
        "+        <em>Statement.</em> The actual state-action sequence satisfies $\\liminf_{T}\\frac1T\\sum_{t\\lt T}r_i(s_t,a_t)\\ge c_i$ almost surely for every constraint. At completed epochs, $\\liminf_{N\\to\\infty}\\mathbb E\\big[\\frac1{NT_0}\\sum_{t\\lt NT_0}r_0(s_t,a_t)\\big]\\ge P^\\star-\\eta_\\lambda mB^2/2$. If a separate finite bound $B_0\\ge0$ satisfies $|r_0(s_t,a_t)|\\le B_0$ almost surely for every time $t$, then the all-time objective guarantee is $\\liminf_{T\\to\\infty}\\mathbb E\\big[\\frac1T\\sum_{t\\lt T}r_0(s_t,a_t)\\big]\\ge P^\\star-\\eta_\\lambda mB^2/2$. This additional objective bound controls unfinished epochs. If the actual switched expected-average sequence $\\mathbb E\\big[\\frac1T\\sum_{t\\lt T}r_0(s_t,a_t)\\big]$ has a finite limit as $T\\to\\infty$, that limit satisfies the same lower bound under A1--A3 and the stated integrability assumptions.<br>",
        "         <em>In words.</em> Nothing is claimed about any single policy $\\pi(\\lambda_k)$ being optimal; the guarantee is about the <em>trajectory</em> generated by switching among Lagrangian maximisers as the multipliers drift. In the monitoring problem of Section 3 the multiplier $\\lambda_i$ records the deficit of time spent in $R_i$ relative to $c$ (their Eq. 16); the executed policy at $R_1$ jumps between \"return to $R_0$\" ($\\lambda_1\\lt\\max(1,\\lambda_2)$) and \"stay in $R_1$\" ($\\lambda_1\\gt\\max(1,\\lambda_2)$; a large $\\lambda_1$ alone is not enough, since with $\\lambda_2\\gt\\lambda_1\\gt1$ heading for $R_2$ pays more, and at ties the Lagrangian leaves the choice to the policy's tie-breaking), neither of which solves the problem, while the time averages of the switched trajectory meet both fractions (their Fig. 3). The failure of fixed-$\\lambda$ methods there is one of <em>recovery</em>, not of representation (Exercise 8.6).",
