        "-            <strong>(b)</strong> $\\mathbb E_{\\pi^\\star}[A_c]=2p-2(1-p)=4p-2\\le0.5\\iff p\\le0.625$. With $\\nu=0$: $p=\\sigma(2)=0.881$, violated. Equality: $2(1-2\\nu)=\\ln\\frac{0.625}{0.375}=0.511$, so $\\nu=0.372$ and $p=0.625$. The objective is $\\mathbb E_{\\pi^\\star}[A]=2p-1=0.25$, down from $0.762$ without the cost constraint.<br>",
        "-            <strong>(c)</strong> $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}=0.625\\ln1.25+0.375\\ln0.75=0.1395-0.1079=0.0316\\le0.05$: the trust region is slack. The exact solution of the two-constraint problem is therefore $p=0.625$, with multipliers $(\\lambda,\\nu)=(0,\\,0.5)$ by complementary slackness and stationarity of the (now linear) Lagrangian $2p-1-\\nu(4p-2.5)$; the Gibbs form degenerates at $\\lambda=0$. FOCOPS, which fixes the temperature at $\\lambda=1$, reaches the same policy with $\\nu=0.372$. With a single free parameter $p$, a fixed temperature reproduces it whenever an <em>admissible</em> $\\nu\\ge0$ makes the cost constraint tight: $p=0.625$ requires $\\nu=\\tfrac12-\\tfrac\\lambda4\\ln\\tfrac53$, which is non-negative only for $\\lambda\\le2/\\ln\\tfrac53\\approx3.915$. For hotter temperatures the unconstrained Gibbs policy already has $p=\\sigma(2/\\lambda)\\lt0.625$ (e.g. $0.583$ at $\\lambda=6$), so $\\nu=0$ and FOCOPS takes a smaller step than the exact solution. The multiplier values are not comparable across temperatures.<br>",
        "-            <strong>(d)</strong> $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p)=p\\ln2p+(1-p)\\ln2(1-p)$ increases for $p\\gt\\tfrac12$ and reaches $0.02$ at $p=0.5997$ (check: $0.6\\ln1.2+0.4\\ln0.8=0.0201$). Now the KL constraint binds first: $4p-2=0.399\\lt0.5$ leaves the cost constraint slack, so $\\nu^\\star=0$, and the temperature solves $\\sigma(2/\\lambda)=0.5997$, i.e. $2/\\lambda=\\ln\\frac{0.5997}{0.4003}=0.404$, $\\lambda^\\star\\approx4.95$. It indeed minimises the dual $\\lambda\\delta+\\lambda\\ln\\cosh(1/\\lambda)$: the derivative $\\delta+\\ln\\cosh(1/\\lambda)-\\tanh(1/\\lambda)/\\lambda$ vanishes there. Moral: the two multipliers take turns; FOCOPS in practice fixes $\\lambda$, adapts only $\\nu$, and enforces $\\delta$ by rejecting states and stopping early.",
        "+            <strong>(b)</strong> $\\mathbb E_{\\pi^\\star}[A_c]=2p-2(1-p)=4p-2\\le0.5\\iff p\\le0.625$. With $\\nu=0$: $p=\\sigma(2)\\approx0.881$, violated. Equality: $2(1-2\\nu)=\\ln\\frac{0.625}{0.375}=\\ln\\tfrac53\\approx0.511$, so $\\nu=\\tfrac12-\\tfrac14\\ln\\tfrac53\\approx0.372$ and $p=0.625$. The objective is $\\mathbb E_{\\pi^\\star}[A]=2p-1=0.25$, down from $2\\sigma(2)-1\\approx0.762$ without the cost constraint.<br>",
        "+            <strong>(c)</strong> $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}=0.625\\ln1.25+0.375\\ln0.75=\\tfrac58\\ln\\tfrac54-\\tfrac38\\ln\\tfrac43\\approx0.0316\\lt0.05$: the trust region is slack. The exact solution of the two-constraint problem is therefore $p=0.625$, with multipliers $(\\lambda,\\nu)=(0,\\,0.5)$ by complementary slackness and stationarity of the (now linear) Lagrangian $2p-1-\\nu(4p-2.5)$; the Gibbs form degenerates at $\\lambda=0$. FOCOPS with the temperature fixed at $\\lambda=1$ reaches the same policy with $\\nu=\\tfrac12-\\tfrac14\\ln\\tfrac53\\approx0.372$. With a single free parameter $p$, a fixed temperature reproduces it whenever an <em>admissible</em> $\\nu\\ge0$ makes the cost constraint tight: $p=0.625$ requires $\\nu=\\tfrac12-\\tfrac\\lambda4\\ln\\tfrac53$, which is non-negative only for $\\lambda\\le2/\\ln\\tfrac53\\approx3.915$. For hotter temperatures the unconstrained Gibbs policy already has $p=\\sigma(2/\\lambda)\\lt0.625$ (e.g. $p\\approx0.583$ at $\\lambda=6$), so $\\nu=0$ and FOCOPS takes a smaller step than the exact solution. The multiplier values are not comparable across temperatures.<br>",
        "+            <strong>(d)</strong> $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p)=p\\ln2p+(1-p)\\ln2(1-p)$ increases for $p\\gt\\tfrac12$. Let $p^\\star$ be its unique root $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p^\\star)=0.02$ in $[\\tfrac12,\\tfrac58]$; $0.59966520\\lt p^\\star\\lt0.59966521$, so $p^\\star\\approx0.5997$ (check: $0.6\\ln1.2+0.4\\ln0.8\\approx0.0201\\gt0.02$). Now the KL constraint binds first: $4p^\\star-2\\approx0.399\\lt0.5$ leaves the cost constraint slack, so $\\nu^\\star=0$, and the temperature solves $\\sigma(2/\\lambda^\\star)=p^\\star$, i.e. $2/\\lambda^\\star=\\ln\\frac{p^\\star}{1-p^\\star}\\approx0.404$, $\\lambda^\\star=2/\\ln\\frac{p^\\star}{1-p^\\star}\\approx4.95$. It indeed minimises the dual $\\lambda\\delta+\\lambda\\ln\\cosh(1/\\lambda)$: the derivative $\\delta+\\ln\\cosh(1/\\lambda)-\\tanh(1/\\lambda)/\\lambda$ vanishes there. Moral: the two multipliers take turns; FOCOPS in practice fixes $\\lambda$, adapts only $\\nu$, and applies per-state KL rejection and sampled-average KL early stopping.",
