        "@@ -855,3 +855,3 @@\n",
        "             <strong>(b)</strong> For $\\theta\\le1$, $F_\\kappa=(\\theta-2)^2$ is decreasing. For $\\theta\\gt1$, $F_\\kappa'(\\theta)=2(\\theta-2)+\\kappa$ vanishes at $\\theta=2-\\kappa/2$. If $\\kappa\\lt2$ this point lies above 1 and is the minimiser, infeasible by $1-\\kappa/2$ (e.g. $\\kappa=1$: $\\theta=1.5$). If $\\kappa\\ge2$, $F_\\kappa$ is increasing on $\\theta\\gt1$ and $\\bar\\theta=1$ is the unique minimiser. Threshold: $\\kappa\\ge\\bar\\lambda=2$ (at $\\kappa=2$ the minimiser is still unique here because $f$ is strictly convex).<br>",
        "-            <strong>(c)</strong> <em>Quadratic penalty:</em> for $\\theta\\gt1$, $2(\\theta-2)+\\mu(\\theta-1)=0$ gives $\\theta_\\mu=\\frac{4+\\mu}{2+\\mu}$, infeasible by $\\frac{2}{2+\\mu}$ for every finite $\\mu$ ($\\mu=10$: $0.167$; $\\mu=100$: $0.020$). <em>Barrier:</em> for $\\theta\\lt1$, $2(\\theta-2)+\\frac{1}{t(1-\\theta)}=0$; with $u=1-\\theta$ this is $2u^2+2u=1/t$, so $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{1+2/t}-1}{2}\\approx\\frac{1}{2t}$. The iterate is strictly feasible and the objective gap is $f(\\theta_t)-f(\\bar\\theta)=(1+u)^2-1=2u+u^2=\\frac1t-u^2\\le\\frac1t$: exactly IPO's $m/t$ bound with $m=1$ ($t=10$: $0.098\\le0.1$). The implied multiplier $\\lambda_t=\\frac{1}{tu}$ equals $2.10$ at $t=10$ and $2.01$ at $t=100$, converging to $\\bar\\lambda=2$: the barrier follows the central path. Only the ReLU penalty is exact at a finite weight, and that weight is the unknown $\\bar\\lambda$; hence P3O's schedule $\\kappa\\leftarrow\\min(\\rho\\kappa,\\kappa_{\\max})$.<br>",
        "+            <strong>(c)</strong> <em>Quadratic penalty:</em> for $\\theta\\gt1$, $2(\\theta-2)+\\mu(\\theta-1)=0$ gives $\\theta_\\mu=\\frac{4+\\mu}{2+\\mu}$, infeasible by $\\frac{2}{2+\\mu}$ for every finite $\\mu$ ($\\mu=10$: $0.167$; $\\mu=100$: $0.020$). <em>Barrier:</em> for $\\theta\\lt1$, $2(\\theta-2)+\\frac{1}{t(1-\\theta)}=0$; with $u=1-\\theta$ this is $2u^2+2u=1/t$, so $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{1+2/t}-1}{2}\\approx\\frac{1}{2t}$. The iterate is strictly feasible and the objective gap is $f(\\theta_t)-f(\\bar\\theta)=(1+u)^2-1=2u+u^2=\\frac1t-u^2\\le\\frac1t$: exactly IPO's $m/t$ bound with $m=1$ ($t=10$: $0.098\\le0.1$). The implied multiplier $\\lambda_t=\\frac{1}{tu}=1+\\sqrt{1+2/t}$ is approximately $2.10$ at $t=10$ and $2.01$ at $t=100$, converging to $\\bar\\lambda=2$: the barrier follows the central path. Only the ReLU penalty is exact at a finite weight, and that weight is the unknown $\\bar\\lambda$; hence P3O's schedule $\\kappa\\leftarrow\\min(\\rho\\kappa,\\kappa_{\\max})$.<br>",
        "             <strong>(d)</strong> Minimise $-\\theta$ subject to $\\theta\\le0$: $\\bar\\theta=0$, $\\bar\\lambda=1$. With $\\kappa=1$, $F(\\theta)=-\\theta+\\max\\{0,\\theta\\}=\\max\\{-\\theta,0\\}$ attains its minimum $0$ at every $\\theta\\ge0$, including every infeasible $\\theta\\gt0$; for $\\kappa\\gt1$ the minimiser $\\theta=0$ is unique, for $\\kappa\\lt1$ there is none. This is why part (b) of the theorem in Section 4 needs $\\kappa\\gt\\|\\bar\\lambda\\|_\\infty$.",
        "@@ -872,3 +872,3 @@\n",
        "             <strong>(a)</strong> At the end of training, with the per-environment ratio averaged over 18 environments and 3 seeds, CPO's excess episode cost $\\max(0,J_c-25)$ was 59.3% of the excess of <em>unconstrained</em> PPO. Zero would mean constraint satisfaction; the Lagrangian values $0.026$ and $0.018$ are small positive violations; no uncertainty interval is supplied.<br>",
        "-            <strong>(b)</strong> Treat this as a hypothetical task whose ratios equal the reported SG18 averages; the aggregate does not determine any individual task's cost. Then $\\rho_c=\\bar\\rho_c\\,\\rho_c^E$: PPO-Lagrangian $0.0245$, i.e. $24.5\\le25$ per episode on average (just); TRPO-Lagrangian $0.0265$, i.e. $26.5$ (slightly above); CPO $0.0646$, i.e. $64.6$. The break-even reference would be $\\rho_c^E=0.025/0.245=0.102$ for PPO-Lagrangian and $0.039$ for CPO. (Averages of ratios are not ratios of averages, so this is only an order-of-magnitude reading.)<br>",
        "+            <strong>(b)</strong> Treat this as a hypothetical task whose ratios equal the reported SG18 averages; the aggregate does not determine any individual task's cost. Then $\\rho_c=\\bar\\rho_c\\,\\rho_c^E$: PPO-Lagrangian $0.0245$, i.e. $24.5\\le25$ per episode on average (just); TRPO-Lagrangian $0.0265$, i.e. $26.5$ (slightly above); CPO $0.0646$, i.e. $64.6$. The break-even reference would be $\\rho_c^E=0.025/0.245=5/49\\approx0.102$ for PPO-Lagrangian and $0.025/0.646=25/646\\approx0.039$ for CPO. (Averages of ratios are not ratios of averages, so this is only an order-of-magnitude reading.)<br>",
        "             <strong>(c)</strong> $\\bar M_c$ is measured at the end of training; $\\rho_c$ integrates every cost over the whole run, including early exploration before the multiplier has grown. A Lagrangian agent that starts unsafe and becomes safe late has a small $\\bar M_c$ and a large $\\bar\\rho_c$. This is why Safety Gym proposes the cost rate as a measure of safety regret, and why later work reports safety <em>during</em> learning.<br>",
