        "-            <strong>(c)</strong> Algorithm 1 samples a cost target $\\kappa\\in[c_{\\min},c_{\\max}]$ and a reward target $\\rho$ above what the data achieve at that cost, finds $\\tau^\\ast=\\arg\\max\\{R(\\tau):C(\\tau)\\le\\kappa\\}$, here $(6,5)$, and appends a copy of $\\tau^\\ast$ whose reward-to-go tokens are shifted element-wise by $\\rho-R(\\tau^\\ast)=+3$ and whose cost-to-go tokens are shifted by $\\kappa-C(\\tau^\\ast)=+5$. The model thus learns to answer the infeasible request $(9,10)$ with the actions of the best safe trajectory: the cost token gets priority. CDT states as its goal a policy whose best return at threshold $\\kappa$ is at least $\\mathrm{PF}(\\kappa)$; this is an aim supported by experiments, not a theorem.<br>",
        "-            <strong>(d)</strong> Costs are non-negative and the relabelling only adds $\\kappa-C(\\tau^\\ast)\\ge0$, so every cost-to-go token in the training data is non-negative: a negative $C_t$ is out of distribution and the model's behaviour is undefined (the sequence-model analogue of CPQ's out-of-distribution actions). Relabelling covers targets at the start of an episode, not overshoots in the middle of one; the stochastic head and entropy term reduce compounding errors but do not remove them. A hard per-episode budget needs the state augmentation of <a href=\"cmdp.html#state-augmentation\">Module 8</a> (Sauté RL) or FISOR-style feasibility."
