        "-        <em>Assumption 2.</em> $\\|f(x,u+d)-f(x,u)\\|\\le\\lambda\\|d\\|_\\infty$ for $x\\in X$ and $u,\\,u+d\\in U$.<br>",
        "+        <em>Assumption 1 (local incremental stabilisability).</em> There are a feedback $\\kappa:X\\times X\\times U\\to\\mathbb R^m$, a <em>$\\delta$-Lyapunov function</em> $V_\\delta:X\\times X\\times U\\to\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$ continuous in its first state argument, with $V_\\delta(z,z,v)=0$ for $z\\in X$, $v\\in U$, and constants $c_{\\delta,l},c_{\\delta,u},\\delta_{\\rm loc},k_{\\max}\\gt0$, $\\rho\\in(0,1)$ such that, for $(x,z,v)\\in X\\times X\\times U$ and $(z^+,v^+)\\in X\\times U$, whenever $V_\\delta(x,z,v)\\le\\delta_{\\rm loc}$,",
        "+        $$c_{\\delta,l}\\|x-z\\|^2\\le V_\\delta(x,z,v)\\le c_{\\delta,u}\\|x-z\\|^2,\\qquad\\|\\kappa(x,z,v)-v\\|\\le k_{\\max}\\|x-z\\|,\\qquad x^+\\in X,\\qquad V_\\delta(x^+,z^+,v^+)\\le\\rho\\,V_\\delta(x,z,v),$$",
        "+        with $x^+=f(x,\\kappa(x,z,v))$ and $z^+=f(z,v)$: a feedback that lets the true state $x$ track a nominal trajectory $z$ driven by inputs $v$, the tracking certificate $V_\\delta$ shrinking by the factor $\\rho$ per step. Here we adopt the typed-domain reading of Assumption 1: the contraction clause includes $x^+\\in X$, so that its left-hand side is defined on the stated domain of $V_\\delta$. The paper does not display this membership as a separate clause; an extension of $V_\\delta$ to the full state space would need a separate successor-domain hypothesis. This supplies a local one-step domain condition; shifted-plan feasibility is proved separately. Unadorned vector norms here are Euclidean; the disturbance norm $\\|d\\|_\\infty$ is the infinity input norm.<br>",
        "+        <em>Assumption 2.</em> Choose a positive Lipschitz bound $\\lambda\\gt0$ with $\\|f(x,u+d)-f(x,u)\\|\\le\\lambda\\|d\\|_\\infty$ for $x\\in X$ and $u,\\,u+d\\in U$.<br>",
        "         <em>Assumption 3.</em> $\\eta\\le\\lambda^{-1}\\sqrt{\\delta_{\\rm loc}/c_{\\delta,u}}$.<br>",
        "-        <em>Tightening (eq. 9).</em> With $X=\\{x:Hx\\le\\mathbf 1\\}$ and $U_t=\\{u:L_tu\\le\\mathbf 1\\}$: <span class=\"math-scroll\">$\\epsilon:=\\eta\\lambda\\sqrt{c_{\\delta,u}/c_{\\delta,l}}\\,\\max\\{\\|H\\|_\\infty,\\|L_t\\|_\\infty k_{\\max}\\}$</span> and $\\epsilon_k=\\epsilon\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\\sqrt\\rho^{\\,j}$.<br>",
        "+        <em>Tightening (eq. 9).</em> With $X=\\{x:Hx\\le\\mathbf 1\\}$ and $U_t=\\{u:L_tu\\le\\mathbf 1\\}$: <span class=\"math-scroll\">$\\epsilon:=\\eta\\lambda\\sqrt{c_{\\delta,u}/c_{\\delta,l}}\\,\\max\\{\\|H\\|_\\infty,\\|L_t\\|_\\infty k_{\\max}\\}$</span> and $\\epsilon_k=\\epsilon\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\\sqrt\\rho^{\\,j}$. Define the tightened sets by the paper&rsquo;s explicit row inequalities: $\\bar X_k=\\{x:Hx\\le(1-\\epsilon_k)\\mathbf 1\\}$ and $\\bar U_k=\\{u:L_tu\\le(1-\\epsilon_k)\\mathbf 1\\}$. For $1-\\epsilon_k\\gt0$, these equal $(1-\\epsilon_k)X$ and $(1-\\epsilon_k)U_t$; scalar-multiplication notation is used here only for positive factors. The row definitions also specify zero or negative right-hand sides, without assuming $\\epsilon_k\\le1$.<br>",
        "         <em>Assumption 4 (terminal set).</em> $V_f(x)=x^\\top Px$, $X_f=\\{V_f\\le\\alpha_f\\}$ and a controller $k_f$ with, for all $x\\in X_f$: $f(x,k_f(x))+w\\in X_f$ for every $\\|w\\|\\le\\lambda\\eta\\sqrt{\\rho^Nc_{\\delta,u}/c_{\\delta,l}}$; $V_f(f(x,k_f(x)))\\le V_f(x)-\\big(x^\\top Qx+k_f(x)^\\top Rk_f(x)\\big)$; $(x,k_f(x))\\in\\bar X_N\\times\\bar U_N$.<br>",
        "-        <em>In words.</em> An input error $\\|d\\|_\\infty\\le\\eta$ moves the next state by at most $\\lambda\\eta$ (Assumption 2), so the re-planned trajectory starts within $V_\\delta\\le c_{\\delta,u}\\lambda^2\\eta^2\\le\\delta_{\\rm loc}$ of the old plan: Assumption 3 is exactly what keeps this inside the region where the tracking certificate holds. Afterwards $\\sqrt{V_\\delta}$ shrinks by $\\sqrt\\rho$ per step, so $k$ steps later the state is within $\\sqrt\\rho^{\\,k}\\lambda\\eta\\sqrt{c_{\\delta,u}/c_{\\delta,l}}$ of the old plan; in constraint units (factors $\\|H\\|_\\infty$, and $\\|L_t\\|_\\infty k_{\\max}$ for inputs) this is at most $\\epsilon\\sqrt\\rho^{\\,k}=\\epsilon_{k+1}-\\epsilon_k$, the increment of the tightening. What is left after $N$ steps is the ball the terminal set must absorb. All of this is checked before learning; validation only estimates how often the network stays within $\\eta$.",
        "+        <em>In words.</em> An input error $\\|d\\|_\\infty\\le\\eta$ moves the next state by at most $\\lambda\\eta$ (Assumption 2). The first disturbed successor must first be kept in $X$ by the constraint tightening. Continuity of $V_\\delta$ along the segment from the nominal successor (where it is zero) then lets the local quadratic upper bound establish $V_\\delta\\le c_{\\delta,u}\\lambda^2\\eta^2\\le\\delta_{\\rm loc}$ without assuming its own locality premise. At each subsequent contraction step the state and shifted-input domain conditions must also hold. Under those conditions $\\sqrt{V_\\delta}$ shrinks by $\\sqrt\\rho$ per step, so $k$ steps later the state is within $\\sqrt\\rho^{\\,k}\\lambda\\eta\\sqrt{c_{\\delta,u}/c_{\\delta,l}}$ of the old plan; in constraint units (factors $\\|H\\|_\\infty$, and $\\|L_t\\|_\\infty k_{\\max}$ for inputs) this is at most $\\epsilon\\sqrt\\rho^{\\,k}=\\epsilon_{k+1}-\\epsilon_k$, the increment of the tightening. What is left after $N$ steps is the ball the terminal set must absorb. All of this is checked before learning; validation only estimates how often the network stays within $\\eta$.",
