        "-        <details style=\"margin:10px 0;\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;color:#2d5fa6;font-weight:600;\">Worked solution</summary><div style=\"padding:8px 14px;background:#f7f9fc;border-left:3px solid #2d5fa6;\"><p><strong>Step 1.</strong> $S_2^\\top S_2=(0.98^2+0.2^2)I=1.0004I$, so $\\|S_2\\|_2=\\sqrt{1.0004}\\approx1.00019998$, slightly above one.</p><p><strong>Step 2.</strong> The skew-exponential remainder bound is $\\|e^K-S_2\\|_2\\le0.2^3/6=0.00133333$. Since $e^K$ is orthogonal, $\\|S_2\\|_2\\le1.00133333$, a valid looser upper bound.</p><p><strong>Step 3.</strong> For ten layers the remainder-based product bound is $(1+0.2^3/6)^{10}\\approx1.01341362$. Exact layer norms give $(\\sqrt{1.0004})^{10}\\approx1.00200160$. Intermediate activations can reduce the true gain, but calling the truncated network exactly 1-Lipschitz would be unjustified.</p></div></details>",
        "+        <details style=\"margin:10px 0;\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;color:#2d5fa6;font-weight:600;\">Worked solution</summary><div style=\"padding:8px 14px;background:#f7f9fc;border-left:3px solid #2d5fa6;\"><p><strong>Step 1.</strong> $S_2^\\top S_2=(0.98^2+0.2^2)I=1.0004I$, so $\\|S_2\\|_2=\\sqrt{1.0004}\\approx1.00019998$, slightly above one.</p><p><strong>Step 2.</strong> The skew-exponential remainder bound is $\\|e^K-S_2\\|_2\\le0.2^3/6\\approx0.00133333$. Since $e^K$ is orthogonal, $\\|S_2\\|_2\\le1+0.2^3/6$. The rounded bound $\\|S_2\\|_2\\le1.00133333$ is also valid here by the exact norm in Step 1.</p><p><strong>Step 3.</strong> For ten layers the remainder-based product bound is $(1+0.2^3/6)^{10}\\approx1.01341362$. Exact layer norms give $(\\sqrt{1.0004})^{10}\\approx1.00200160$. Intermediate activations can reduce the true gain, but calling the truncated network exactly 1-Lipschitz would be unjustified.</p></div></details>",
